  "response": "The administrative consequences follow a separate logic, and the record on them admits of only one resolution: either no student's test scores at the school were invalidated, or the state invalidated the test scores of 142 students at the school. Exactly one of those outcomes occurred, and the two cannot both stand. Settling which one obtained requires the documentary trail of the agency's final determination rather than any further statistical modeling, which is why careful treatments of such episodes separate the measurement question from the question of what sanction was ultimately imposed.\n\nThe quantitative record at the school in question is stark. The school's wrong-to-right erasure rate exceeded the state average by more than three standard deviations, a departure that constitutes strong evidence that irregular administration inflated the figures the school reported. Adjudication, however, need not end with a single winner: at least one of two things is the case, and perhaps both at once \u2014 the new instructional program raised actual student achievement at the school, and irregularities in test administration inflated the school's reported scores.\n\nThe statistical detection of administration irregularities has a well-developed methodological lineage. Brian Jacob and Steven Levitt built a detection algorithm that combines unusual answer-string patterns with unexpected score fluctuations, flagging classrooms whose response profiles depart from what honest test-taking produces. Their analysis of New York City public school data is precisely what yielded that detection method, since those records supplied the variation from which the algorithm was constructed.\n\nThe school's reported proficiency rate rose from 35 percent to 78 percent in a single year, and that leap led the state education agency to commission a forensic audit of the school's answer documents. A single-year jump of that magnitude is what sets the comparative work in motion: researchers compare gains on the high-stakes test with gains on a low-stakes assessment given to the same students, on the reasoning that genuine learning should travel across instruments. Difference-in-differences designs, by contrast, leave individual students aside and compare the school's score trajectory with that of similar schools that did not adopt the reform. Genuine instructional reform tends to produce gains concentrated in the skills targeted by the new curriculum, so the profile of item-level improvement carries diagnostic weight. The commissioning of the forensic audit is also what brought erasure analysis to bear, a technique that measures the frequency of wrong-to-right answer changes on scanned answer sheets. Whereas that measurement is a matter of tallying marks on paper, the technique by contrast identifies which specific individual altered each changed answer.",
